[36m(TaskRunner pid=266095)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, I need to write a function called check_date that takes month, day, and year as integers and returns True if the date is valid, False otherwise. Let\'s think about the steps involved here.\n\nFirst, I should consider the basic validations. The month must be between 1 and 12. The day depends on the month and whether it\'s a leap year for February. \n\nWait, right. For February, the days vary between 28 and 29. So I need a way to check if the year is a leap year. How do I determine that? The leap year rules are: divisible by 4, but not by 100 unless also divisible by 400. So like 2000 is a leap year, but 1900 isn\'t.\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n1. Check if the month is in 1-12. If not, return False.\n2. Check the day based on the month. For months with 31 days (like January, March, etc.), check day is 1-31. For April, June, Sept, Nov (30 days), check 1-30. For February, check 1-28 or 29 depending on leap year.\n\nBut how to handle the leap year check. Let me write a helper function is_leap_year(year) that returns True if it\'s a leap year.\n\nWait, but the problem says the function should take three integers as parameters. So the code will be in Python.\n\nSo the code structure would be:\n\ndef check_date(month, day, year):\n    # Check month\n    if month < 1 or month > 12:\n        return False\n    # Check day based on month\n    if month in [1,3,5,7,8,10,12]:\n        max_day = 31\n    elif month in [4,6,9,11]:\n        max_day = 30\n    else: # February\n        if is_leap_year(year):\n            max_day = 29\n        else:\n            max_day = 28\n    # Check day is within 1 to max_day\n    if day < 1 or day > max_day:\n        return False\n    return True\n\nThen implement the is_leap_year function.\n\nWait, but the problem says the function returns True if the date is valid. So the helper function is needed.\n\nBut wait, what about the year? The Gregorian calendar starts in 1582, but maybe the problem doesn\'t require handling dates before that. However, the problem says "Gregorian calendar date", so perhaps we don\'t need to check for invalid years. The function\'s parameters are integers, so even if the year is 0 or negative, the function should check if the date is valid. But the problem statement doesn\'t specify, so perhaps the function just checks the month and day as per the rules, regardless of the year.\n\nSo the code would proceed as above.\n\nBut let me think about the edge cases. For example, February 29, 2020 is valid, but February 29, 2021 is not.\n\nSo the helper function:\n\ndef is_leap_year(year):\n    if year % 4 != 0:\n        return False\n    elif year % 100 != 0:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return year % 400 == 0\n\nYes, that\'s correct.\n\nNow, putting it all into the check_date function.\n\nBut I need to make sure that the code follows the company\'s coding rules. The variables should be descriptive, add comments for non-obvious steps.\n\nSo in the code, variables like max_day are clear. The months are checked with clear conditions.\n\nAnother thing: what about the day being zero? The function returns False for day < 1.\n\nSo the code should handle that.\n\nNow, coding this into Python. Also, the function is named check_date, and parameters are month, day, year.\n\nSo the final code would be:\n\ndef check_date(month, day, year):\n    """\n    Validates if the given month, day, and year form a valid Gregorian calendar date.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    month (int): The month (1-12).\n    day (int): The day (1-31 depending on the month).\n    year (int): The year (any integer, but must be valid for February\'s leap year check).\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # Check if the month is valid (1-12)\n    if month < 1 or month > 12:\n        return False\n\n    # Determine the maximum number of days in the given month\n    if month in {1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}:\n        max_day = 31\n    elif month in {4, 6, 9, 11}:\n        max_day = 30\n    else:  # February\n        # Check for leap year to determine days in February\n        if is_leap_year(year):\n            max_day = 29\n        else:\n            max_day = 28\n\n    # Check if the day is within the valid range\n    if day < 1 or day > max_day:\n        return False\n\n    return True\n\ndef is_leap_year(year):\n    """\n    Determines if the given year is a leap year in the Gregorian calendar.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    year (int): The year to check.\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the year is a leap year, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # A leap year is divisible by 4, but not by 100 unless also divisible by 400.\n    if year % 4 != 0:\n        return False\n    elif year % 100 != 0:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return year % 400 == 0\n\nBut wait, the problem says that the function should be named check_date and take the three parameters. The code above does that, and the helper function is_leap_year is inside the same module. But in Python, the helper function needs to be defined before check_date uses it. So the order is important.\n\nBut since the code is written in a single file, the helper function can be placed after check_date, but in Python, the functions are processed in the order they are defined. So the code should define is_leap_year before check_date, or have check_date call it after it\'s defined.\n\nWait, in the code I wrote above, check_date calls is_leap_year, which is defined after check_date. That\'s a problem. So the order needs to be adjusted.\n\nSo the correct order is to define is_leap_year first, then check_date.\n\nSo the code should be:\n\ndef is_leap_year(year):\n    # ... as before ...\n\ndef check_date(month, day, year):\n    # ... as before ...\n\nBut the problem says the function is named check_date. So the code should have the helper function inside, or as a separate function. However, the problem requires that the function check_date is written, and the helper function is_leap_year is part of the solution.\n\nBut the problem says that the solution should be in experiments/solution.py. So the code will be written as the check_date function, with the helper function.\n\nSo in the code, the helper function is defined first, then check_date.\n\nAnother thing: the problem states that the function should return True if the date is valid. So all the conditions are covered.\n\nNow, I need to write the code in experiments/solution.py, following the rules. The variables names are descriptive, comments explain the steps, and the code is self-documenting.\n\nI think that\'s it. Now, I need to create the solution.py file in experiments/ directory.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\nThe solution involves implementing a function to validate a date according to the Gregorian calendar. The function checks if the month is valid, determines the maximum number of days for the given month (considering leap years for February), and verifies if the day falls within the valid range.\n\n```python\ndef is_leap_year(year):\n    """\n    Determines if the given year is a leap year in the Gregorian calendar.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    year (int): The year to check.\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the year is a leap year, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # A leap year is divisible by 4, but not by 100 unless also divisible by 400.\n    if year % 4 != 0:\n        return False\n    elif year % 100 != 0:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return year % 400 == 0\n\n\ndef check_date(month, day, year):\n    """\n    Validates if the given month, day, and year form a valid Gregorian calendar date.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    month (int): The month (1-12).\n    day (int): The day (1-31 depending on the month).\n    year (int): The year (any integer, but must be valid for February\'s leap year check).\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # Check if the month is valid (1-12)\n    if month < 1 or month > 12:\n        return False\n\n    # Determine the maximum number of days in the given month\n    if month in {1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}:  # Months with 31 days\n        max_day = 31\n    elif month in {4, 6, 9, 11}:  # Months with 30 days\n        max_day = 30\n    else:  # February (handle leap year)\n        if is_leap_year(year):\n            max_day = 29\n        else:\n            max_day = 28\n\n    # Check if the day is within the valid range\n    if day < 1 or day > max_day:\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```\n</answer>\n\n<bash>echo \'def is_leap_year(year):\n    """\n    Determines if the given year is a leap year in the Gregorian calendar.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    year (int): The year to check.\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the year is a leap year, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # A leap year is divisible by 4, but not by 100 unless also divisible by 400.\n    if year % 4 != 0:\n        return False\n    elif year % 100 != 0:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return year % 400 == 0\n\n\ndef check_date(month, day, year):\n    """\n    Validates if the given month, day, and year form a valid Gregorian calendar date.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    month (int): The month (1-12).\n    day (int): The day (1-31 depending on the month).\n    year (int): The year (any integer, but must be valid for February\'s leap year check).\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # Check if the month is valid (1-12)\n    if month < 1 or month > 12:\n        return False\n\n    # Determine the maximum number of days in the given month\n    if month in {1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}:  # Months with 31 days\n        max_day = 31\n    elif month in {4, 6, 9, 11}:  # Months with 30 days\n        max_day = 30\n    else:  # February (handle leap year)\n        if is_leap_year(year):\n            max_day = 29\n        else:\n            max_day = 28\n\n    # Check if the day is within the valid range\n    if day < 1 or day > max_day:\n        return False\n\n    return True\' > ./experiments/solution.py</bash>'
