. Which is correct.\n\nAh, I see. So the previous code missed the masking step. That's why the example didn't work. So the correct way is to mask the result to the same number of bits as the original number.\n\nSo, in code:\n\nmask = (1 << bits) - 1\nrotated = ((number << effective_count) | (number >> (bits - effective_count))) & mask\n\nSo updating the code:\n\ndef left_Rotate(number, count):\n    if number == 0:\n        return 0\n    bits = number.bit_length()\n    effective_count = count % bits\n    if effective_count == 0:\n        return number\n    mask = (1 << bits) - 1\n    shifted_left = number << effective_count\n    shifted_right = number >> (bits - effective_count)\n    return (shifted_left | shifted_right) & mask\n\nTesting this with number=9 (1001), count=1. bits=4, effective_count=1. mask=15 (1111). shifted_left is 18 (10010), shifted_right is 1 (0001). combined is 10011 (19). mask is 15, so 19 & 15 is 3 (0011). Correct.\n\nAnother example: number=15 (1111), count=1. bits=4. effective_count=1. shifted_left is 30 (11110), shifted_right is 15 >> 3 = 1. OR is 30 | 1 = 31. mask is 15. 31 & 15 is 15. Which is correct because rotating left 1 bit on 1111 would give 1111 again (since all bits are the same). So that's correct.\n\nAnother example: number=5 (101), count=1. bits=3. effective_count=1. mask is 7 (111). shifted_left is 10 (1010), shifted_right is 5 >> (3-1) = 5 >> 2 = 1 (001). OR is 10 | 1 = 11 (3 in decimal). Which is 011, but with mask 7, it's 3. Wait, but 5 is 101, left rotate once: 011 (3), which is correct.\n\nYes. So the code with the mask is correct.\n\nNow, what about if the number is zero? Well, if number is zero, we return 0. That's handled.\n\nBut what about if the count is larger than the bits? For example, number=5 (101, 3 bits), count=4. effective_count = 4 mod 3 = 1. So the same as rotating once. Which is correct.\n\nAnother edge case: count is zero. Then the function returns the original number.\n\nNow, handling the case where numbe